<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napoleonic Code</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Napoleonic Code is the French civil code issued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1804, codifying law on persons, property and contracts. It was exported across continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">First Empire</a>'s period of dominance. Through European states and their colonies it shaped legal systems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, one of the French Empire's most enduring legacies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Code" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DerHexer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Speyer,_Historical_Museum_of_the_Palatinate_-_Code_civil_des_Fran%C3%A7ais.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the Revolution France had no single body of law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">Civil law</a> consisted mainly of local customs, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/custom-of-paris">Custom of Paris</a>, together with exemptions, privileges and special charters granted by kings and lords. On 4 September 1791 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> resolved that there should be a code of civil laws common to the whole realm, and in 1793 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> set up a commission under Jean-Jacques-Régis de Cambacérès. His drafts of 1793, 1794 and 1796 were all rejected, one as too technical, one as too brief and one never discussed. A fourth outline, partly by Jean-Ignace Jacqueminot, was also rejected.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Battle of Marengo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> appointed a new commission of jurists on 12 August 1800, among them Jacques de Maleville, François Denis Tronchet, Félix-Julien-Jean Bigot de Préameneu and Jean-Étienne-Marie Portalis. Napoleon did not draft the Code himself, but he and Cambacérès chaired many of the plenary sessions. The Legislative Body rejected the bill by 142 votes to 139 in December 1801. Napoleon then suspended all projects in January 1802 and pressed the legislature into submission, and the Code came into effect on 21 March 1804.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Code borrowed its categories not from French law but from Justinian's Corpus Juris Civilis. It was divided into persons, property, the acquisition of property, and civil procedure, which was moved into a separate code in 1806. Unlike Justinian's work it was a comprehensive rewrite, rationally arranged, written in the vernacular and without religious content. Its preliminary article required laws to be promulgated and published, forbade secret and retroactive laws, and barred judges from refusing justice because the law was insufficient while forbidding them to lay down general rules. On the family, it set the husband above his wife and children, and divorce by mutual consent was abolished in 1804.</p><p>Other Napoleonic codes followed: the code of civil procedure in 1806, the commercial code in 1807, the code of criminal procedure in 1808 and a criminal code in 1810. Article 294 of the code of criminal procedure allowed the defendant access to a lawyer before the Cour d'assises.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Code was not the first civil code in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> had earlier ones, but it was the first modern code adopted with a pan-European scope. It strongly influenced the law of many countries formed during and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><p>West of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and in Berg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> it applied until the German Civil Code of 1900, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> adopted a code with its influence in 1864. It is still in force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, heavily amended, and is the main source of law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>. Louisiana's civil code also shows French influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Napoleonic Code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, exported across continental Europe during Napoleon's brief continental dominance, fundamentally shaped the legal systems of France's European neighbours and, through them and through French colonial rule, those of states across Africa and the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Constitution of 1987, amended in 2012, made a semi-presidential republic: a president elected for five years without consecutive terms, a prime minister from the majority in the National Assembly, a two-chamber National Assembly and a modified <b>Napoleonic Code</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Boyer centralised government in Port-au-Prince, issued a civil code modelled on the <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, and relied on army commanders who ran each district and on a small circle of mixed-race officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon, ruling by force, carried the civil equality and secular law of the <b>Napoleonic Code</b> of 1804 across much of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman penal code of 1858, modelled on France’s <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, abolished punishments such as amputation of a limb for theft and decriminalized homosexuality; it endured until 1928.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1807 abolished personal serfdom, declared equality before the law and introduced the <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, though nobles kept their land.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-warsaw">Duchy of Warsaw</a></li></ul></section>
